WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:06.851
You already use a moral principle you have probably never said out loud.

2
00:00:06.851 --> 00:00:10.650
You use it every week, with the people you care about most.

3
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:16.753
Think about the last serious disagreement you had with someone close to you.

4
00:00:16.753 --> 00:00:17.970
Not where to eat.

5
00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:22.092
Money, a child, a decision that would shape both your lives.

6
00:00:22.092 --> 00:00:24.119
You were sure you were right.

7
00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:24.998
So were they.

8
00:00:25.898 --> 00:00:30.689
And whatever you did, there is one thing you almost certainly did not do.

9
00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:34.833
You did not find someone with the authority to make them comply.

10
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:37.494
Suppose you could have.

11
00:00:37.494 --> 00:00:41.749
One call, and a person with real power over them arrives,

12
00:00:41.749 --> 00:00:44.024
with penalties if they refuse.

13
00:00:44.024 --> 00:00:45.711
The disagreement ends.

14
00:00:45.711 --> 00:00:46.298
You win.

15
00:00:47.198 --> 00:00:49.437
Almost nobody takes that offer.

16
00:00:49.437 --> 00:00:52.166
And notice how fast they turn it down.

17
00:00:52.166 --> 00:00:54.406
It doesn't feel like a dilemma.

18
00:00:54.406 --> 00:00:55.595
It feels obvious.

19
00:00:56.495 --> 00:00:59.563
Ask people why not, and the answers come fast.

20
00:00:59.563 --> 00:01:01.717
It would wreck the relationship.

21
00:01:01.717 --> 00:01:02.891
They'd resent me.

22
00:01:02.891 --> 00:01:05.828
They'd go along without changing their mind.

23
00:01:05.828 --> 00:01:08.504
I wouldn't trust myself with that power.

24
00:01:08.504 --> 00:01:12.747
Every one of those is a claim about what force does to a person.

25
00:01:12.747 --> 00:01:16.662
Not a theory; something you learned by watching what worked.

26
00:01:17.562 --> 00:01:19.427
Persuasion builds something.

27
00:01:19.427 --> 00:01:24.763
Force produces compliance, which looks like the same thing right up until you need

28
00:01:24.763 --> 00:01:26.177
the person to want it.

29
00:01:27.077 --> 00:01:28.303
Now scale it up.

30
00:01:28.303 --> 00:01:32.700
When millions disagree about healthcare, schooling, or land,

31
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.666
the system that settles it does not run on persuasion.

32
00:01:36.666 --> 00:01:37.891
It runs on force.

33
00:01:38.791 --> 00:01:39.959
Pass a law.

34
00:01:39.959 --> 00:01:40.738
Compel.

35
00:01:40.738 --> 00:01:43.950
Fine or imprison whoever refuses.

36
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.452
The people on the receiving end are as certain of their reasons,

37
00:01:48.452 --> 00:01:52.332
and as capable of resentment, as the person you were just thinking of.

38
00:01:53.232 --> 00:01:56.529
Politics is full of persuasion, and that's real.

39
00:01:56.529 --> 00:02:00.566
But when the process ends, the outcome applies to everyone,

40
00:02:00.566 --> 00:02:04.065
including the people who argued against it and lost.

41
00:02:04.965 --> 00:02:09.360
In your own life, failing to persuade someone means you don't get your way.

42
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.858
In politics, failing to persuade someone means you get your way anyway.

43
00:02:15.758 --> 00:02:21.579
The Philosophy of Human Respect makes one observation and follows it: the pattern

44
00:02:21.579 --> 00:02:25.484
you already rely on does not stop being true at scale.

45
00:02:25.484 --> 00:02:31.376
Happiness, harmony and prosperity always decrease with the initiation of violence,

46
00:02:31.376 --> 00:02:36.132
or the taking of people's property or time through force or fraud.

47
00:02:36.132 --> 00:02:39.326
They grow where people cooperate voluntarily.

48
00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.758
So carry the question.

49
00:02:44.226 --> 00:02:48.871
If persuasion is the method you trust in every relationship where you can see the

50
00:02:48.871 --> 00:02:53.628
results, what is the argument for building our shared institutions on the other one?
